    "proved": "Arbitrary compact complex auxiliary factors and smooth semipositive vertical terms preserve the separated curve envelope identities, complete geodesic polar metric, ideal analytic closure and nearest-point ideal retraction without an ambient mass bound. Zero-obstacle equality, atomic polar curvature, relative-envelope membership and equality at every smooth obstacle remain equivalent. All model comparison volumes vanish. Vanishing auxiliary terms or curve areas force nonbigness; positive curve areas and strictly positive vertical terms give bigness, including genuinely nonclosed examples.",
    "mechanism": "Genuine curve-product slices and countable-divisor domination compute the base projections. Unit-divisor reduction transfers them upstairs and gives zero mass for every ordinary representative. The common potential on Y times Z identifies the polar and actual analytic loci across auxiliary factors. Pullback of curve stretching competitors proves the obstacle criterion. Compact-fiber descent and closed top-degree auxiliary test forms prove the nonbig cases; smooth curve gauges prove bigness, and the mixed derivative component proves genuine nonclosedness.",
    "retained_boundary": "Only the specified product locus is metrized; its top-volume comparison expression is identically zero. The all-obstacle criterion holds for the stated pulled-back curve potentials. No unrestricted ordinary completion or mixed-obstacle coordinate formula is claimed."
  },
